    "question": {
      ":question/id": "8d8d4cc1-2100-5bf7-ac1b-1610efaefe75",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186133881/assets/edb891f50e2954edfa2f9ab93e21e29b7dc29d58668b15570f35c05888eee525.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$\n- **II.** $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$\n- **III.** $\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the negative $z$-direction.\n- **IV.** $F$ is irrotational",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\,F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that $F$ represents the velocity field of flowing water. Place a paddlewheel at $P$ with its axis in the $z$-direction. If it rotates, the field has nonzero curl; if it does not, the field has zero curl.\n\nThe arrows turn a paddlewheel clockwise. Thus $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$ throughout space, so $F$ is not irrotational. Curl measures local rotation, even when the arrows also spread outward. Wrap the fingers of your right hand in the clockwise direction: your thumb points into the screen, in the negative $z$-direction.\n\nWith that in mind, check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false.\n- Statement II is true.\n- Statement III is true.\n- Statement IV is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
